1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,940
Welcome back. Now that you

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:04,860
understand how
essential tracking is,

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,090
let's explore and compare

4
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:08,265
various types of
tracking methods.

5
00:00:08,265 --> 00:00:10,530
The purpose of your
project plan is to

6
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:12,945
guide you through the
execution of your project.

7
00:00:12,945 --> 00:00:14,865
Therefore, it will always include

8
00:00:14,865 --> 00:00:16,890
at least one type
of tracking method,

9
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:18,660
and sometimes you
might use more than

10
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:21,015
one depending on what
your team needs.

11
00:00:21,015 --> 00:00:23,025
The tracking methods
we'll discuss

12
00:00:23,025 --> 00:00:24,900
in this video are a Gantt chart,

13
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,450
a roadmap, and a burndown chart.

14
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:28,755
There are many others,

15
00:00:28,755 --> 00:00:31,365
but these are the few that
I'm going to focus on.

16
00:00:31,365 --> 00:00:33,690
Whichever type you
choose depends on

17
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:35,910
what you deem suitable
for your project.

18
00:00:35,910 --> 00:00:38,490
It's important to remember
to select something

19
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:41,235
that the entire team
can easily understand,

20
00:00:41,235 --> 00:00:43,560
reference, and keep up to date.

21
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:46,315
Let's compare the tracking
methods I mentioned.

22
00:00:46,315 --> 00:00:48,440
Perhaps the most
common tracking method

23
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:50,360
of all is the Gantt chart.

24
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,900
It's a tried and true tool to
keep your project on track.

25
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,135
A Gantt chart measures tasks

26
00:00:56,135 --> 00:00:58,850
against time and includes
useful information,

27
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:00,740
like who will own each task and

28
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,980
what the order of
the tasks should be.

29
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:05,330
For this reason,
it's a useful chart

30
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:07,520
for staying on schedule
and for projects with

31
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,620
many dependencies or tasks or

32
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:12,905
activities or milestones that
are reliant on one another.

33
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:14,420
It's also a helpful chart for

34
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:16,205
teams with a lot
of people, because

35
00:01:16,205 --> 00:01:17,900
ownership and
responsibilities are

36
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,044
explicitly laid out visually.

37
00:01:20,044 --> 00:01:22,925
As you track and move along
sequentially over time,

38
00:01:22,925 --> 00:01:24,830
this starts to look
like a waterfall;

39
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:26,840
hence, why Gantt
charts are commonly

40
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:29,305
used in Waterfall
project management.

41
00:01:29,305 --> 00:01:33,110
Each task is represented by a
horizontal progress bar, and

42
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:35,000
the length of the
bar is dependent on

43
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,025
how much time is
allotted to the task.

44
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:38,630
The bars are stacked on top of

45
00:01:38,630 --> 00:01:40,400
each other to denote
that the task at

46
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:42,200
the top must be completed before

47
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,210
the next one below
it can be completed.

48
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:45,890
Gantt charts typically live in

49
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:47,135
your project plan and are

50
00:01:47,135 --> 00:01:49,465
updated as the
project progresses.

51
00:01:49,465 --> 00:01:51,680
Another common tracking
method we have

52
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:53,770
here at Google is a roadmap.

53
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:56,210
A roadmap is best suited
for when you need

54
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:59,090
a way to track big
milestones in your project.

55
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:00,650
It's useful for illustrating

56
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:02,150
how a project should evolve over

57
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:06,420
time to a team and key
stakeholders. Here's an example:

58
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:09,650
a roadmap might list your
project's goals at the top and

59
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:11,030
a description of the approach

60
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:12,670
we'll be taking to
meet those goals.

61
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:15,125
In this example, the
goals are to increase

62
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:18,550
online business-to-consumer
sales by 20 percent year-over-year

63
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:20,750
and to increase

64
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:22,760
holiday sales to
existing customers

65
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:24,880
by eight percent over last year.

66
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:27,590
The approach details
the main tactics

67
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,010
your team will use
to reach your goal.

68
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:34,210
A roadmap also includes a
high-level project overview.

69
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:37,685
High-level in this context
means a concise summary,

70
00:02:37,685 --> 00:02:40,430
usually three-to-four
sentences, to clearly state

71
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:43,060
the objectives and
priorities for your project.

72
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:44,925
Below the goals, approach,

73
00:02:44,925 --> 00:02:46,520
and overview is a table that

74
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:48,880
maps out what the
process will entail.

75
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,350
In this example, our
table is divided into

76
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:53,900
quarters that reflect
the project timeline.

77
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:56,165
A quarter is a three-month period

78
00:02:56,165 --> 00:02:58,345
on a company's
financial calendar.

79
00:02:58,345 --> 00:03:01,235
Key milestones are
listed for each quarter.

80
00:03:01,235 --> 00:03:02,990
Then, there are tasks that

81
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:05,885
each team member or
department needs to complete.

82
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:07,850
Most tasks may map to

83
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:10,490
a milestone due within
the same quarter.

84
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:13,520
In our example, the
project as a whole has

85
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:15,530
a key milestone in Q1 to

86
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:18,155
finalize inventory for
the holiday season.

87
00:03:18,155 --> 00:03:19,865
The work of product testing

88
00:03:19,865 --> 00:03:21,620
and finalizing suggestions for

89
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:25,345
offerings mainly falls to the
marketing and sales teams.

90
00:03:25,345 --> 00:03:28,910
However, sometimes the tasks
may need to be completed in

91
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:31,100
advance to unblock another team

92
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,055
or milestone in future quarters.

93
00:03:33,055 --> 00:03:35,390
The product and engineering
team is working

94
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:37,640
on tasks in Q1 and in

95
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:39,560
Q2 that lead up to the launch of

96
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:42,685
the refreshed online store in Q3.

97
00:03:42,685 --> 00:03:44,750
In order to reach that milestone,

98
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:47,990
each department needs to
complete specific tasks,

99
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:50,465
and the roadmap tracks
both individual

100
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:53,305
and project progress
toward milestones.

101
00:03:53,305 --> 00:03:55,605
Finally, there's
the burndown chart,

102
00:03:55,605 --> 00:03:58,245
the most granular of the
three tracking methods.

103
00:03:58,245 --> 00:04:01,250
A burndown chart measures
time against the amount of

104
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:04,255
work done and the amount
of work remaining.

105
00:04:04,255 --> 00:04:07,610
Their main uses are to keep
the project team on top of

106
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,680
targeted completion
dates and to keep

107
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:12,820
the team aware of scope
creep as it occurs.

108
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:16,010
Burndown charts are best suited
for projects that require

109
00:04:16,010 --> 00:04:18,305
a detailed, broken-down review of

110
00:04:18,305 --> 00:04:20,735
each task associated
with a project,

111
00:04:20,735 --> 00:04:22,730
and they're great
for projects where

112
00:04:22,730 --> 00:04:25,595
finishing on time is
the top priority.

113
00:04:25,595 --> 00:04:28,579
The y-axis or the vertical axis

114
00:04:28,579 --> 00:04:31,925
symbolizes the number of
tasks left to complete,

115
00:04:31,925 --> 00:04:36,760
and the x-axis or the
horizontal axis signifies time.

116
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:38,360
Progress gets tracked from

117
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:40,535
the upper left-hand
corner of the chart.

118
00:04:40,535 --> 00:04:42,185
As the project goes along,

119
00:04:42,185 --> 00:04:44,000
you'll track down, working your

120
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,355
way towards zero remaining tasks,

121
00:04:46,355 --> 00:04:47,645
and to the right,

122
00:04:47,645 --> 00:04:50,060
working your way
toward your end date.

123
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:52,070
There's typically a dotted line

124
00:04:52,070 --> 00:04:53,869
for your expected or projected

125
00:04:53,869 --> 00:04:55,850
progress based on the rate your

126
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:58,125
team's expected to close tasks,

127
00:04:58,125 --> 00:05:01,895
and a solid line representing
your actual progress.

128
00:05:01,895 --> 00:05:04,460
Before you continue
on with your project,

129
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:07,460
you'll need to decide which
tracker makes the most sense.

130
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:10,505
If you need to communicate
milestones to a large team,

131
00:05:10,505 --> 00:05:11,810
you might choose a roadmap.

132
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:14,045
If you have a project with
multiple dependencies,

133
00:05:14,045 --> 00:05:15,485
you might choose a Gantt chart.

134
00:05:15,485 --> 00:05:17,150
If tracking tasks against

135
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:19,025
your deadline is
especially important,

136
00:05:19,025 --> 00:05:21,610
then the burndown chart
might be your best option.

137
00:05:21,610 --> 00:05:24,500
And you may even decide
to use more than one.

138
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,135
Because the tracking method

139
00:05:26,135 --> 00:05:27,500
will be determined by the type

140
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,110
of project you're working
on, your resources,

141
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:31,445
and the project scope,

142
00:05:31,445 --> 00:05:33,680
you'll probably end up
using several types of

143
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:36,175
tracking methods at some
point in your career.

144
00:05:36,175 --> 00:05:37,505
In my time at Google,

145
00:05:37,505 --> 00:05:39,305
I've regularly combined methods

146
00:05:39,305 --> 00:05:41,635
and used a few different
types together.

147
00:05:41,635 --> 00:05:43,970
For example, using a Gantt chart

148
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:46,400
to scope work at the
beginning of a project,

149
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:48,440
then switching to a
burndown chart in

150
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:51,550
the weeks before a launch to
make sure we're good to go.

151
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:53,600
Now you know a bit more about

152
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,190
the various types of
tracking methods,

153
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:58,355
including a roadmap, Gantt
chart, and burndown chart.

154
00:05:58,355 --> 00:06:00,680
Next step, we'll focus
on understanding

155
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:04,080
continuous improvement
and process improvement.
